ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

================================================================================
CUSTOM MODIFICATIONS AND ENHANCEMENTS
================================================================================

This xv6 implementation includes the following custom modifications to enhance
process scheduling, system monitoring, and shell functionality:

1. PRIORITY-BASED PROCESS SCHEDULING
   ====================================
   
   Implemented a priority-based scheduler that prioritizes processes based on
   their priority level.
   
   Files Modified:
   - kernel/proc.h: Added 'int priority' field to struct proc
     Priority range: 0 (highest) to 31 (lowest)
   
   - kernel/proc.c:
     * allocproc(): Initializes each new process with default priority of 20
     * scheduler(): Modified to track and select the highest priority runnable
                   process (lowest numeric value) for CPU execution
   
   How It Works:
   - CPU always executes the process with lowest priority number
   - Prevents lock starvation by ensuring no process waits indefinitely
   - Falls back to idle (wfi) when no runnable processes exist

2. PROCESS INFORMATION SYSCALL (getprocs)
   ======================================
   
   New system call to retrieve information about all running processes,
   enabling user-space tools to monitor system state.
   
   Files Modified:
   - kernel/syscall.h: Added SYS_getprocs definition (syscall #22)
   
   - kernel/syscall.c:
     * Added extern declaration for sys_getprocs()
     * Added entry in syscalls[] array for syscall dispatch
   
   - kernel/sysproc.c: Implemented sys_getprocs() function
     * Scans entire process table
     * Collects: pid, ppid, priority, state, process name
     * Copies data to user-space memory
     * Returns number of processes collected (max 64)
   
   - user/user.h:
     * Added struct procinfo with: pid, ppid, priority, state, name
     * Added getprocs() syscall declaration

3. PROCESS LISTING UTILITY (ps command)
   ===================================
   
   New user-space utility to display all running processes in a formatted table.
   
   New File: user/ps.c
   - Displays process information in table format:
     * PID: Process ID
     * PPID: Parent Process ID
     * Priority: Process priority (0-31)
     * State: Process state (used, sleep, runbl, run, zombie)
     * Name: Process name
   
   - Handles up to 64 processes simultaneously
   - Maps state numbers to readable names for users
   
   Files Modified:
   - Makefile: Added $U/_ps to UPROGS list for compilation

4. SHELL ENHANCEMENTS (sh.c)
   ========================
   
   Significantly improved the interactive shell with modern command-line features.
   
   New Features:
   
   a) COMMAND HISTORY
      - Records last 10 commands executed
      - Each command stored with max 100 characters
      - Circular buffer implementation (oldest entry overwritten when full)
      - Access history with 'history' command
      - Type 'history' in shell to view all stored commands
      - Each history entry numbered 1-10, displayed in order
   
   b) TAB COMPLETION
      - Auto-completion support for 26 common xv6 commands
      - Built-in commands: cd, history, exit, wait
      - Utilities: echo, cat, ls, mkdir, rm, ln, kill, grep, wc, ps
      - Kernel ops: fork, pipe, stressfs, usertests, grind, zombie, 
                   logstress, forphan, dorphan, init, sh, forktest
      - Completion behavior:
        * Unique match → Auto-completes instantly
        * 2-5 matches → Lists suggestions, redraws prompt
        * No match or >5 matches → Silent (no action)
   
   c) IMPROVED INPUT HANDLING
      - Character-by-character input processing (real-time TAB detection)
      - Full backspace support with visual feedback
      - Ctrl+D (EOF) handling for proper shell exit
      - All commands automatically logged to history
   
   d) HISTORY COMMAND
      - Type 'history' to display numbered list of recent commands
      - Shows all stored commands from oldest to newest
      - Running 'history' command doesn't interfere with history tracking
   
   Files Modified:
   - user/sh.c: Complete enhancement with:
     * History buffer (10 commands, 100 chars each)
     * Command array (26 common commands)
     * add_history() - Adds command to circular buffer
     * show_history() - Displays all stored commands
     * find_matches() - Finds commands matching prefix for completion
     * getcmd_with_completion() - Enhanced input handler with TAB/backspace
     * Modified main() - Integrates history, history command, completion
   
   Preserved Functionality:
   - All existing shell features work unchanged
   - Pipe operations (|) fully compatible
   - Input/output redirection (>, <, >>) unaffected
   - Background job execution (&) works normally
   - cd command functionality preserved
   - Process execution and command parsing unchanged

5. BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY
   ======================
   
   All modifications maintain full backward compatibility:
   - Existing processes unaffected by priority scheduling
   - Default priority (20) suitable for normal workloads
   - New syscall (getprocs) doesn't interfere with existing code
   - ps command is optional; existing tools still function
   - Shell improvements additive; basic shell still works without TAB/history

================================================================================
TESTING THE NEW FEATURES
================================================================================

1. Build the modified xv6:
   $ make clean
   $ make

2. Test Priority Scheduler:
   - Start multiple processes and observe CPU allocation
   - Modify process priority values and recompile to observe change in scheduling

3. Test Process Listing (ps):
   $ ps
   Will display table with all running processes and their properties

4. Test Shell History:
   $ history
   Shows last 10 commands executed in numeric order

5. Test Tab Completion:
   - Type any command prefix (e.g., 'e') and press TAB
   - System will auto-complete 'echo' if unique
   - Type 'g' and press TAB to see: grep, grind, grun options

6. Verify Backward Compatibility:
   - All existing commands still work: ls, cat, mkdir, etc.
   - Pipes work: $ cat file | grep pattern
   - Redirects work: $ ls > listing.txt
   - Background jobs work: $ sleep 10 &

